---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import IncomeTaxCalculator from '@components/islands/IncomeTaxCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'income-tax-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How is federal income tax calculated?', answer: 'The U.S. uses progressive brackets: different portions of your income are taxed at increasing rates. Your income is first reduced by the standard (or itemized) deduction, then each bracket applies only to the income that falls within its range. This calculator uses the 2024 brackets and standard deduction.' },
  { question: 'Which states have no income tax?', answer: 'Nine states levy no tax on wage income: Alaska, Florida, Nevada, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington, Wyoming and New Hampshire. (New Hampshire taxed interest and dividends until that tax was fully repealed in 2025, and Washington taxes certain capital gains but not wages.) In every other state you also owe state income tax on top of the federal figure shown here.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between the marginal and effective rate?', answer: 'Your marginal rate is the rate on your last dollar of income — your top bracket. Your effective rate is your total tax divided by your total income, and it is always lower, because your first dollars are taxed in the cheaper brackets. A "22% bracket" earner usually pays an effective rate closer to 12–15%.' },
  { question: 'Is Social Security taxed as income?', answer: 'Two separate things. While you work, wages fund Social Security and Medicare through FICA payroll tax (7.65%), which is separate from income tax. In retirement, up to 85% of your Social Security benefits can be taxable as income depending on your total income — many lower-income retirees owe nothing on them.' },
  { question: 'When is the income tax deadline?', answer: 'Federal returns are normally due April 15. If the 15th falls on a weekend or holiday it moves to the next business day, and you can request an automatic extension to file (though not to pay) until October 15. State deadlines usually mirror the federal date.' },
  { question: 'Does this include state tax, FICA and credits?', answer: 'No — this is a federal income tax estimate only. It excludes state and local income taxes, FICA payroll taxes, and credits such as the Earned Income Tax Credit or Child Tax Credit, which can substantially lower (or, for refundable credits, reverse) your actual bill.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate your U.S. federal income tax using the 2024 brackets and standard deduction, with your effective and marginal rates and after-tax income."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-09' }}
>
  <IncomeTaxCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your <strong>gross annual income</strong> — total pay before any tax is taken out.</li>
      <li>Choose your <strong>filing status</strong>: single or married filing jointly. Each uses its own bracket table and standard deduction.</li>
      <li>Optionally add <strong>pre-tax deductions</strong> such as traditional 401(k) or HSA contributions, which come off your income on top of the standard deduction.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see your estimated federal tax, taxable income, after-tax income, and both your marginal and effective rates.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The result is a federal-only estimate for the 2024 tax year. It applies the standard
      deduction automatically, so you enter gross income and the tool handles the deduction
      and the bracket-by-bracket arithmetic.
    </p>

    <h2>How progressive tax brackets work</h2>
    <p>
      A higher salary does not push your <em>whole</em> income into a higher rate — only the
      portion that falls inside each bracket is taxed at that bracket's rate. That is the
      single most misunderstood part of the tax system, and it is why your
      <strong>effective</strong> rate (total tax ÷ income) is always lower than your
      <strong>marginal</strong> rate (your top bracket). These are the 2024 federal rates for
      a single filer, applied to income after the standard deduction:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tax rate</th><th>Taxable income (single filer, 2024)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>10%</td><td>$0 – $11,600</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12%</td><td>$11,600 – $47,150</td></tr>
        <tr><td>22%</td><td>$47,150 – $100,525</td></tr>
        <tr><td>24%</td><td>$100,525 – $191,950</td></tr>
        <tr><td>32%</td><td>$191,950 – $243,725</td></tr>
        <tr><td>35%</td><td>$243,725 – $609,350</td></tr>
        <tr><td>37%</td><td>Over $609,350</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Single vs. married filing jointly</h2>
    <p>
      Married-filing-jointly couples use the same seven rates but over roughly doubled bands,
      and a larger standard deduction ($29,200 versus $14,600 for 2024). Because the lower
      brackets stretch twice as far, a couple with one earner keeps more than a single filer
      on the identical income:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tax rate</th><th>Taxable income (married filing jointly, 2024)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>10%</td><td>$0 – $23,200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12%</td><td>$23,200 – $94,300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>22%</td><td>$94,300 – $201,050</td></tr>
        <tr><td>24%</td><td>$201,050 – $383,900</td></tr>
        <tr><td>32%</td><td>$383,900 – $487,450</td></tr>
        <tr><td>35%</td><td>$487,450 – $731,200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>37%</td><td>Over $731,200</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      On a $120,000 income, a single filer owes about $18,339, while a married-jointly filer
      with the same income owes roughly $10,432 — the wider bands and bigger deduction cut the
      bill by nearly half. Switch the filing status above to see the difference on your own
      numbers.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take a single filer earning <strong>$60,000</strong>. Subtract the 2024 standard
      deduction of $14,600 and taxable income is <strong>$45,400</strong>. The tax is built up
      one bracket at a time:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>10% on the first $11,600 = <strong>$1,160</strong></li>
      <li>12% on the remaining $33,800 = <strong>$4,056</strong></li>
      <li>Total federal tax ≈ <strong>$5,216</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That $45,400 sits entirely inside the 12% bracket, so 12% is the marginal rate — but the
      $5,216 bill is only <strong>8.7%</strong> of the $60,000 salary, because the first
      $11,600 was taxed at just 10% and the deduction escaped tax entirely.
    </p>

    <h2>Marginal vs. effective rate</h2>
    <p>
      The gap between the two rates widens as income climbs. A single filer on $120,000 reaches
      the <strong>24% marginal</strong> bracket, yet pays an <strong>effective rate of about
      15.3%</strong> — because only the slice above $100,525 is taxed at 24%, while earlier
      dollars were taxed at 10%, 12% and 22%. When people say they are "in the 24% bracket,"
      they almost never pay 24% of their income; the effective rate is the number that reflects
      what you actually owe.
    </p>

    <h2>How deductions lower your bill</h2>
    <p>
      Taxable income is gross income minus deductions, and this tool always applies the
      <strong>standard deduction</strong> for your filing status. Traditional 401(k) and HSA
      contributions reduce taxable income further, and each dollar removed is saved at your
      <em>marginal</em> rate. Add $5,000 of pre-tax 401(k) to the $60,000 example and taxable
      income drops to $40,400, cutting the tax to about $4,616 — a $600 saving, exactly 12% of
      the $5,000.
    </p>
    <p>
      Deductions differ from <em>credits</em>, which the tool does not model. A deduction lowers
      the income that gets taxed; a credit cuts the tax itself, dollar for dollar. The Earned
      Income Tax Credit and Child Tax Credit are the most common, and refundable credits can
      exceed the tax withheld and produce a net refund.
    </p>

    <h2>What this estimate leaves out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>State and local income tax.</strong> Only federal tax is shown; most states add their own on top. Nine — Alaska, Florida, Nevada, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington, Wyoming and New Hampshire — tax no wage income.</li>
      <li><strong>FICA payroll tax.</strong> Social Security and Medicare (7.65% of wages) are separate from income tax and are not included here.</li>
      <li><strong>Credits and itemized deductions.</strong> The tool uses the standard deduction and no credits, so an actual return with children, education costs or large itemized deductions can differ substantially.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Treat the figure as a planning estimate for the 2024 tax year, not a filing number; for an
      official calculation, consult a tax professional or the IRS. To see your pay in other
      terms, use the <a href="/finance/salary-calculator">salary calculator</a>; to plan pre-tax
      retirement contributions, try the <a href="/finance/retirement-calculator">retirement
      calculator</a>. This is general educational information, not tax advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
